## 2. Back-of-Envelope Scale Estimation

This is the step that separates ICT4 from ICT5 answers. Do this math out loud.

| Quantity | Estimate | Reasoning |
|---|---|---|
| PDFs | 500,000,000 | given |
| Avg pages/PDF | ~15 | reasonable assumption for mixed reports/manuals/contracts |
| Total pages | **7.5 billion** | 500M × 15 |
| Avg chunks/page | ~3 (≈400–600 tokens/chunk with overlap) | semantic/recursive chunking, not naive fixed-size |
| **Total chunks (vectors)** | **~22.5 billion** | 7.5B × 3 |
| Embedding dim | 768–1024 | typical for a strong retrieval model (e.g. E5-large, BGE-large) |
| Raw embedding storage (fp32) | 22.5B × 768 × 4B ≈ **69 TB** | before compression |
| Raw embedding storage (int8/PQ) | ~2–5 TB | after quantization — this is why quantization is non-negotiable at this scale |
| Raw PDF blob storage | 500M × ~2MB avg ≈ **1 PB** | object storage, not the bottleneck (cheap, S3-class) |
| Ingestion throughput needed (30-day backfill) | 500M / 30 / 86400 ≈ **~193 PDFs/sec**, ~2,900 pages/sec | sustained parsing+chunking+embedding throughput |
| Steady-state new docs (assume 1% churn/day) | ~5M PDFs/day ≈ 58 PDFs/sec | ongoing ingestion after backfill |

**Takeaway to say out loud:** "At 22 billion vectors, a single-node ANN index is off the table — HNSW graphs of this size don't fit in memory on any single machine, and even IVF-based indexes need to be sharded. This tells me the entire retrieval layer has to be a distributed, sharded ANN system, not 'pick a vector DB and call it done.'" This sentence alone signals ICT5-level thinking.

