## 4. Deep Dive: Ingestion Pipeline

### 4.1 PDF Parsing

PDFs are not one thing. At 500M scale you **will** have a long tail of garbage: scanned faxes, password-protected files, corrupted files, PDFs that are actually images, multi-column academic papers, forms, spreadsheets exported as PDF.

| PDF type | Strategy |
|---|---|
| Native text (selectable) | Direct text extraction (e.g. PyMuPDF/pdfplumber-class tools) — cheap, fast |
| Scanned/image-only | OCR pipeline (e.g. Tesseract/cloud OCR) — 10-50x more expensive, GPU/CPU heavy |
| Mixed layout (tables, multi-column, figures) | Layout-aware parsing model (detects reading order, table structure) |
| Tables | Extract as structured data, not flattened text — preserve row/column semantics for chunking |
| Images/diagrams | Optionally caption via a vision model if visual content is queryable |

**Design decision to state explicitly:** run a fast **triage classifier** first (is this native text or scanned?) so you don't route every PDF through expensive OCR — this alone can cut ingestion compute by 5-10x since most enterprise PDFs are native text.

### 4.2 Chunking Strategy

(You've already built deep notes on this — the short version for the interview:)

- **Fixed-size chunking** is the naive baseline — fast but breaks semantic units mid-sentence/mid-table.
- **Recursive/structure-aware chunking** (split on headings → paragraphs → sentences, respecting document structure) is the industry default for this scale because it's cheap and mostly-good.
- **Semantic chunking** (embed sentences, group by similarity) gives higher retrieval quality but costs significantly more compute — **not worth it at 22B-chunk scale as the default**; reserve it for high-value document classes (e.g. legal contracts) if the interviewer probes on quality vs cost trade-offs.
- Chunk size: 400–600 tokens with ~10-15% overlap is the standard sweet spot balancing recall vs precision.
- **Tables need separate handling**: convert to a normalized text representation (e.g. "row: X, column: Y, value: Z") rather than chunking raw table text, or numeric queries silently fail.

### 4.3 Embedding Generation

- Batch inference on GPU workers, not per-document sync calls. At ~2,900 pages/sec × 3 chunks/page ≈ **8,700 chunks/sec** sustained during backfill — this needs a large horizontally-scaled GPU fleet with dynamic batching (e.g. queue-based, Triton/vLLM-style batching).
- Use a **distilled/smaller bi-encoder** for the base embedding model (e.g. a 100-400M param model) — at this scale, a marginally-better-but-3x-slower embedding model is rarely worth it. Save your interviewer-points here for talking about *why* small-and-fast wins at scale, not naming the fanciest model.
- **Idempotency & dedup**: hash raw PDF content (e.g. content hash) before ingestion — with 500M PDFs there will be massive duplication (same manual uploaded by many users/orgs). Dedup at the document level before spending embedding compute.

### 4.4 Metadata Extraction

Store alongside each chunk: `doc_id`, `chunk_id`, `page_num`, `tenant/ACL info`, `source_uri`, `ingestion_timestamp`, `content_hash`. This is what makes citations, freshness, and access control possible later — easy to forget in the whiteboard rush, but interviewers notice its absence.

