## 10. Evaluation & Monitoring

- **Offline**: retrieval metrics (Recall@k, MRR, nDCG) against a labeled eval set; generation metrics (faithfulness/groundedness, answer relevance) — likely worth referencing your existing RAG evaluation knowledge here.
- **Online**: click-through/thumbs-up-down feedback loops, latency percentiles (p50/p95/p99) per stage, index freshness lag (time from PDF upload to searchable), cost-per-query.
- **Golden query set**: maintain a curated regression set of queries with known-correct answers to catch quality regressions on every embedding-model or index change.

---

## 11. Cost Considerations (mention briefly at the end)

- Vector index memory/compute dominates cost at this scale far more than LLM generation cost per query, *if* traffic is high — this is somewhat counterintuitive and worth stating.
- Quantization (§5.1) is as much a cost lever as a performance one.
- Tiered storage: hot/recently-accessed docs in fast index tier, cold long-tail docs in a cheaper, higher-latency tier — most real-world corpora follow a power-law access pattern, so this saves significant infra cost.

---

## 12. Likely Follow-Up Questions (practice these)

1. *"Walk me through what happens if we need to re-embed the entire corpus with a new model."* → Dual-index migration, shadow traffic, gradual cutover, never a big-bang swap on 22B vectors.
2. *"How do you keep the index fresh if a document is updated?"* → Tombstone old chunks, insert new ones, background compaction; explain why in-place HNSW updates are painful (motivates IVF-PQ choice).
3. *"What if a query needs information spread across multiple documents (multi-hop)?"* → Iterative retrieval / query decomposition, or a re-retrieval step conditioned on an intermediate answer.
4. *"How would you reduce hallucination?"* → Grounded prompting + citation requirement + faithfulness scoring + reranking quality (garbage retrieval → garbage generation, so most hallucination fixes actually live in the retrieval stage, not the LLM).
5. *"Single global index vs per-tenant sharding — how do you decide?"* → Query pattern (cross-tenant search needed?), isolation/compliance requirements, and that per-tenant sharding is operationally much simpler at this scale.
6. *"How do you test recall without ground truth for 22B chunks?"* → Sample-based human eval + proxy metrics (e.g., known query-document pairs from click logs) + synthetic query generation from known chunks.

