## 13. One-Paragraph Summary (if asked to compress the whole design)

"I'd split this into an offline ingestion pipeline (parse → chunk → embed → index, tuned for ~193 PDFs/sec sustained throughput) and an online query path (hybrid dense+sparse retrieval → cross-encoder rerank → grounded LLM generation). The core scaling challenge is the ~22 billion chunk vectors, which forces a sharded IVF-PQ index over HNSW for memory and incremental-update reasons, sharded by tenant where possible to avoid expensive scatter-gather. I'd budget latency so retrieval+rerank stays under ~250ms, use RRF to fuse dense and sparse results since dense retrieval alone misses exact-match queries common in PDFs (part numbers, clause references), and treat quantization and dedup as first-class cost levers given the raw embedding footprint would otherwise be ~69TB."

